6 30.910.2427.77 37.88 15.800.5215.98 31.02 31.970.62C/C++68.43 70.74 4.850.0259.39 68.04 2.120.3029.78 39.82 4.780.2773.91 76.81 7.250.0061.11 73.10 1.620.2831.52 44.58 2.970.32Qwen3Java60.11 64.80 4.600.0958.01 68.05 3.230.3828.13 39.81 5.710.3565.57 70.36 4.230.1162.96 73.11 2.260.4026.60 39.40 5.040.38235BPython55.27 63.43 4.030.2448.14 58.02 1.620.4928.66 41.59 5.960.3556.96 65.62 6.750.3353.49 60.66 0.680.4831.43 38.91 6.650.23Average 61.27 66.32 4.490.1155.18 64.70 2.320.3928.86 40.41 5.480.3265.48 70.93 6.080.1559.19 68.96 1.520.3829.85 40.96 4.890.31","level":1,"path_id":"1~71","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":78,"section_id":"sec-1~71"},{"title":"Table 11 :","text":"11Complete dependency source enumeration results reported as Exact Match Rate (EM), Precision (P), Recall (R), and F1 score (%).CORE LiteModelLang.Data DependencyControl DependencyInformation FlowEMPRF1EMPRF1EMPRF1Claude 3.7","level":1,"path_id":"1~72","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":79,"section_id":"sec-1~72"},{"title":"Table 12 :","text":"12Effect of adding reverse dependency reasoning examples to the prompts on dependency classification and trace generation tasks.Language Original Modified PromptLanguage Original Modified PromptC67.6466.86C50.3950.00Java68.1468.34Java44.8645.57Python75.0474.60Python42.3039.32F1 for Dependency Classification","level":1,"path_id":"1~73","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":80,"section_id":"sec-1~73"},{"title":"Table 13 :","text":"13Effect of prompt length reduction on dependency classification and trace generation tasks.Does (varA,lineA) have data dependence over (varB,lineB)? If so, provide one feasible trace.\":Language Original ShortenedLanguage Original ShortenedC67.6467.50C50.3949.80Java68.1468.17Java44.8646.63Python75.0474.04Python42.3039.86F1 for Dependency ClassificationCorrect Trace Rate for Trace Generation(varB,lineB), meaning the value written at (varA,lineA) can propagate to (varB,lineB) through a sequence of read/writeoperations.## 2. Output FormatWhen asked \"\n\t\t\tx = 5\n\t\t\ty = x + 2\n\t\t\tz = y + 3 # z depends on y, and transitively on xHere, y 2 is directly data dependent on x 1 , and z 3 is directly data dependent on y 2 . This forms a transitive data dependency trace: x 1 → D y 2 → D z 3 . Therefore, z 3 has an indirect data dependence on x 1 , denoted as x 1 ⇝ D z 3 .While this example illustrates a simple chain of assignments, real-world programs often exhibit more complex forms of data dependence involving pointers, aliasing, and indirect memory access.\n\t\t\tOriginally from StackOverflow post #16180130.\n\t\t\thttps://github.com/Jur1cek/gcj-dataset\n\t\t\tThe sampled function main, spanning lines 12-40, is from CodeNet problem p00496, solution s700056700.\n\t\t\thttps://github.com/PurCL/LLMSCAN\n\t\t\thttps://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter\n\t\t\thttps://aws.amazon.com/bedrock\n\t\t\thttps://www.deepseek.com/\n\t\t\thttps://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs\n\t\t\thttps://platform.openai.com/docs/models\n\t\t\thttps://openrouter.ai/qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b","level":1,"path_id":"1~74","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":81,"section_id":"sec-1~74"}],"citations":[{"raw_text":"JoshAchiam\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tStevenAdler\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tSandhiniAgarwal\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tLamaAhmad\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tIlgeAkkaya\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tFlorenciaLeoni Aleman\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tDiogoAlmeida\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tJankoAltenschmidt\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tSamAltman\n\t\t\n\t\tarXiv:2303.08774\n\t\tShyamal Anadkat, et al. Gpt-4 technical report\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2023\n\t\t\n\t\n\tarXiv preprint","doi":null,"arxiv_id":"2303.08774","title":null,"anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"GeminiTeam\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tRohanAnil\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tSebastianBorgeaud\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tJean-BaptisteAlayrac\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tJiahuiYu\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tRaduSoricut\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tJohanSchalkwyk\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tAndrewMDai\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tAnjaHauth\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tKatieMillican\n\t\t\n\t\tarXiv:2312.11